<section class="section section--tight">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="answer"><strong>The short answer:</strong> bookkeeping is an activity, and outsourcing is an arrangement. You can have bookkeeping done by your own employee or by an outside provider, and an outside provider may do far more than bookkeeping. So you are really making two decisions: what work you need, and who should do it.</p>

      <h2 id="what">Decision one: what work do you need?</h2>
      <p>These layers build on one another. Each depends on the one before it being right.</p>
      <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Layers of accounting work, scrolls sideways on small screens">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Layer</th><th scope="col">What it means</th><th scope="col">What you get</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Bookkeeping</th><td>Recording sales, purchases, receipts and payments, and reconciling with the bank</td><td>Up-to-date ledgers and party balances</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Accounting</th><td>Adjusting and summarising those records, and preparing financial statements</td><td>Profit and loss account and balance sheet you can rely on</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Compliance</th><td>Preparing and filing GST, TDS, income tax and company returns</td><td>Returns filed, and records that support them</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Analysis</th><td>Reading the numbers: margins, cash flow and budgets</td><td>Decisions you can base on the figures</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>

      <h2 id="who">Decision two: who should do it?</h2>
      <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Who does the work, scrolls sideways on small screens">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Point to compare</th><th scope="col">In-house employee</th><th scope="col">Outsourced provider</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">How the cost behaves</th><td>Fixed salary and on-costs, whatever the workload</td><td>Usually tied to agreed scope and volume</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Daily availability</th><td>On hand for bills, queries and cash</td><td>Works to agreed schedules and channels</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">When someone is away</th><td>Work may stop unless someone can cover</td><td>A firm may have cover. Ask, rather than assume</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Checking of work</th><td>Often no second person</td><td>May have a separate reviewer. Ask who</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Range of skills</th><td>Depends on one person</td><td>May offer more, but may hand work to juniors</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Your supervision</th><td>You manage them</td><td>You manage a relationship and a contract</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Data and access</th><td>Stays inside your business, though still needs controls</td><td>Needs clear terms on storage, access and confidentiality</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>
      <p>These are typical patterns and not rules. A small firm may be one person, and a good employee may be better than a poor provider. Neither arrangement shifts your own legal responsibility for your returns.</p>

      <h2 id="fit">Which situation sounds like yours?</h2>
      <dl class="fit-list">
        <div><dt>A few dozen bills a month and no accounts staff</dt><dd>Outsourced or part-time bookkeeping is usually worth comparing first. A full-time role may sit idle.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Many daily bills, stock movement and walk-in cash</dt><dd>You may need someone on site for recording, with outside help for review and filings.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>An accountant you trust, but returns keep slipping</dt><dd>The gap is probably compliance capacity, not the person. Consider outsourcing only the filing layer and keeping your accountant for the books.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Clean books, and you still cannot tell where the money goes</dt><dd>Your gap is the analysis layer, which more data entry will not fix.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Books are years behind</dt><dd>Start with a defined one-off clean-up, then decide on a regular arrangement.</dd></div>
      </dl>

      <h2 id="mix">The mix many businesses end up with</h2>
      <p>You do not have to choose one for everything. A common pattern is an employee who records daily transactions, with an outside professional who reviews the books, handles filings and takes on what is reserved for qualified people. Write down who owns each layer, so that nothing falls between them.</p>

      <h2 id="switch">If you decide to outsource</h2>
      <p>Before you approach anyone, write down which layers from the first table you are handing over, and which stay with you or your staff. That one page becomes the basis of the written scope, and it stops a provider quoting for less, or more, than you need.</p>
      <p>The handover itself, from choosing a cut-off date to moving logins and the data file into your name, is set out step by step under {% routeLink "/services/outsourced-accounting/", "how an outsourced accounting arrangement works" %}. For choosing the person or provider, read {% routeLink "/guides/how-to-hire-an-accountant-in-delhi/", "how to hire an accountant in Delhi" %}.</p>
